@McClellanOsc That linear scale produces objectives below zero is not a reason to doubt log scale, but to prefer it. Whether measuring rules apply fruitfully to long-term charts is a good question, and not easy to backtest. "The bigger the top, the bigger the drop" is perhaps all we can know.

@McClellanOsc Good point. I don't have a solid explanation for the adage connecting big tops with big drops. There may be a dimensional limit. I do know that institutional traders think in percentage terms. I also know that log scale provides the only consistent measure of long-term amplitude.

@McClellanOsc Comment: Though perhaps independent of a theoretical H&amp;S top, a 30k target is not out of the question given prior cyclical drawdowns measured in percentage terms. https://t.co/GcB5jlk4Td

@McClellanOsc Upshot? I was mainly attempting pun value with my "bears watching" remark, but you raise some good questions. How long does the support/resistance phenomenon last? I'm amused by claims that silver is supported at 50 by its 2011 &amp; 1980 tops. Then again, it did bust out at 50.  🤔

